# =============================================================================================
# Mosquitto access control list -- Factory+ Asset Tracking Platform
# =============================================================================================
#
# DEFAULT IS DENY. Mosquitto grants nothing not matched by a rule below, and `allow_anonymous
# false` in mosquitto.conf means every client authenticates first.
#
# HOW `pattern` WORKS: `%u` is substituted with the connecting client's username, so a gateway
# whose MQTT username is its own `sparkplug_id` can publish only beneath its own edge-node
# segment. That is the rule doing the actual work.
#
# This is the BROKER tier of a two-tier defence; the application tier is
# `verify_gateway_binding()` in ingestion/ingestion.py. Neither is sufficient alone -- the broker
# cannot know which device belongs to which gateway, and the daemon cannot stop a forged message
# reaching other subscribers.
#
# The three attacks this closes, and why a sparkplug_id is not a secret:
#   simulators/README.md -> "Broker topic authorisation"
# =============================================================================================


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-gateway confinement
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Sparkplug B topic is spBv1.0/<group_id>/<message_type>/<edge_node_id>/<device_id>, so
# `spBv1.0/+/+/%u/#` pins the FOURTH segment to the client's own username while leaving the
# group and message type free. A gateway provisioned with username == its `sparkplug_id`
# (see scripts/mosquitto-provision-gateway.mjs) therefore cannot address any other edge node.
#
# Read is granted on the same subtree so a gateway can receive its own Sparkplug command
# topics (NCMD/DCMD) -- and, deliberately, on nothing else: one gateway has no business
# subscribing to another's telemetry.
pattern readwrite spBv1.0/+/+/%u/#

# Sparkplug primary-host STATE topics are a broadcast birth/death for host applications. Every
# edge node needs to read them to know whether the primary host is online; none may write one.
#
# Declared as a global `topic` rule, not a `pattern`: it is the same subtree for every client,
# and Mosquitto warns at startup about a `pattern` carrying no %u/%c substitution -- correctly,
# since such a pattern is just a global rule written misleadingly.
topic read spBv1.0/STATE/#


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backend principal
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The ingestion daemon subscribes to the whole `spBv1.0/#` tree -- that is its job, and it is a
# consumer, not a publisher of asset data.
#
# THIS ACCOUNT IS ALSO STILL USED BY THE DEMO SIMULATOR AND THE E2E VALIDATOR, both of which
# publish as gateways whose credentials cannot be pre-provisioned (validate.py creates its
# gateways at runtime). It therefore retains write access, and is a TRUSTED PRINCIPAL: its
# credential must not be issued to a physical gateway. Moving the simulator and validator onto
# per-gateway credentials is tracked as a follow-up; until then the application tier
# (`verify_gateway_binding`) is what constrains anything using this account.
user factoryplus
topic readwrite spBv1.0/#
# `#` does not match topics beginning with `$`, so the broker-status tree needs naming
# explicitly. The mosquitto healthcheck in docker-compose.yml subscribes to
# `$SYS/broker/version` as this user -- without this line the container never reports healthy
# and every service that depends on it fails to start.
topic read $SYS/#
